  ### 2. Controlling Recursion Depth

  If you want to limit how deep  ugrep  traverses the directory tree, you have multiple options:

  • Using shorthand depth flags ( -1  to  -9 ):
  You can specify a number to restrict recursion to a maximum depth. For example, to search only 2 levels deep:
    ugrep -2 "pattern" /path/to/directory
    (Note:  -1  searches only the top-level files in the specified path without recursing into subdirectories).
  • Using explicit depth ranges ( -m ):
  Use  -m MIN,MAX  or  -m -MAX  to control depth:
    # Search between 3 and 5 levels deep
    ugrep -m 3,5 "pattern" /path/to/directory

    # Search up to a maximum of 4 levels deep
    ugrep -m -4 "pattern" /path/to/directory

  ──────
  ### 3. Including, Excluding, and Ignoring Files

  You can restrict which files or directories  ugrep  scans during recursion using glob patterns:

  • Include only certain files ( -g  or  --include ):
    # Search only within Python (.py) and Markdown (.md) files
    ugrep -r -g "*.py,*.md" "pattern" .

  • Exclude specific files or directories ( --exclude  and  --exclude-dir ):
    # Exclude all .log files and skip the "node_modules" folder
    ugrep -r --exclude="*.log" --exclude-dir="node_modules" "pattern" .

  • Gitignore-style rules ( --ignore-files ):
  Automatically respect  .gitignore  rules (which is great for skipping build folders like  .git ,  dist , or  node_modules ):
    ugrep -r --ignore-files "pattern" .

  ──────
  ### 4. Searching Hidden Files and Compressed Archives

  • Include hidden files/directories ( -.  or  --hidden ):
  By default,  ugrep  skips hidden files and directories (those starting with a dot  . ). Use  -.  to search them:
    ugrep -r -. "pattern" .

  • Recursively search inside archives ( -z ):
  To search inside compressed files (like  .zip ,  .tar.gz ,  .jar , etc.) found during the recursive directory traversal:
    ugrep -rz "pattern" .
    (Use  --zmax=NUM  to control how deep nested archives are unpacked and searched).





